Yep, it is great for pitting monsters against each other while you stand 
back and watch.  But you haven't yet explored the full benefits of 
conflict.  Here's an artificial example using minotaurs.  Even against 
late-game amounts of AC, minotaurs and other dangerous monsters will 
occasionally "roll high" on damage at the same time your damage reduction 
"rolls low", and thus actually harm you, sometimes even significantly.  
Obviously, the more times they swing at you, the more chances they have 
to get lucky.

  HHHHH
  HHHHH
  HH@HH
  HHHHH
  HHHHH

Without conflict, eight minotaurs will swing at you per round.  The 
remaining 16 will patiently stand there, waiting to take the place of any 
you kill.  

With conflict, *one* minotaur will swing at you per round (average).  The 
remaining 23 will quickly tear each other to pieces.  As they die off, 
the chances that adjacent minotaurs swing at you improve, because they 
have less targets, but there will also be less adjacent minotaurs.  
Anyway, by then you can stroll past through the gaps.  The survivors will  
even be too busy to chase you.

Yes, I cheated by using MR 0 monsters.  Sue me.

